WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.789 --> 00:00:03.669
[tense music]

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:05.410
[gunshot]

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.953
It's just unrealistic to
have your hand in front

00:00:07.953 --> 00:00:09.290
and try to shoot under it.

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:11.270
You might shoot your arm off.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:12.570
Hi, I'm Nicole Franks.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.810
I am the women's world fast-draw champion.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.130
I'm from Langley,
British Columbia, Canada.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.188
I also have 50 world records,

00:00:21.188 --> 00:00:23.071
12 world championships,

00:00:23.071 --> 00:00:26.220
and over 50 major championship wins.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.243
And today I'll be rating
fast-draw clips from movies.

00:00:36.041 --> 00:00:37.800
[gunshot]

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.426
In this scene, there's a
lot of staring going on,

00:00:40.426 --> 00:00:43.070
and actually I do this.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.560
I go to my target, and I just stare it up.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.360
And I go --

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.751
When you squint your eyes,

00:00:48.751 --> 00:00:51.550
you typically are locking
in on your target.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.577
And you're just trying to take out

00:00:53.577 --> 00:00:56.890
any peripheral vision in your shooting.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.164
A blink of an eye is actually

00:00:59.164 --> 00:01:02.231
0.26, roughly,

00:01:02.231 --> 00:01:04.100
and that is my fastest world record.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.280
So when they say "blink of an eye,"

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.603
there's a reason to that.

00:01:08.787 --> 00:01:12.710
Clint Eastwood doesn't have
a lot of body movement.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:14.570
He's very straight up and down,

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:17.120
but you can tell that he
actually has shot before,

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.330
because his wrists are very firm.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.480
So when he grabs the gun,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.613
he just grabs the gun

00:01:21.613 --> 00:01:23.780
and he just sort of locks it right in.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.388
And he does shoot out

00:01:25.388 --> 00:01:28.063
a little bit more than I would shoot,

00:01:28.063 --> 00:01:31.738
but he does have just a
complete arm movement.

00:01:31.738 --> 00:01:33.300
It's just super smooth.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.580
There's actually a lot
of cutout in his holster.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:39.880
So, two different style of
holsters back in the day.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.680
Some holsters, the leather would go

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.490
all the way up to the top.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.330
This would make it a lot harder,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.570
because you would have
to pull out a lot further

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.311
to clear the leather.

00:01:50.311 --> 00:01:51.852
Where he has the leather

00:01:51.852 --> 00:01:53.630
almost all cut out for him.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.790
That gives you a lot more room to already

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:59.430
start rotating the gun back
quicker and get it out.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.270
In this scene, you'll see Angel Eyes

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.300
has his holster positioned more so

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.130
to the left of his body.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.265
The reason why people would have this

00:02:07.265 --> 00:02:10.710
is most of the time it's horseback riders.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.830
They would keep it on the left-hand side,

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:14.790
and they would hold the
reins with the other,

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.780
and then anytime an intruder
or something happens,

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.280
they have the option of
grabbing their gun and shooting.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:20.930
When you have it over
to the side of the hip,

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.750
the gun can actually fall out
of the holster a lot more.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:25.820
It was just straight,

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.640
it was just firm, and it was a lock draw.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.400
So I would give him about an eight.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.133
There's nothing pizzazz about it, though.

00:02:37.921 --> 00:02:38.770
[gunshot]

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.336
In this scene, Jamie Foxx starts off

00:02:41.336 --> 00:02:43.036
with his hand off.

00:02:43.036 --> 00:02:44.430
It's the Old West style.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.537
They would intimidate
and move their fingers

00:02:46.537 --> 00:02:50.160
to make people think they were
going to draw ahead of time.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.610
So, some people believe
that hands off is slower

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.380
than hands on.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.350
I personally don't believe that.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.897
If I have hands off, I have
a little bit more of a kick

00:02:59.897 --> 00:03:03.270
than if I'm just going
and grabbing the gun.

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:04.697
It's a little bit more of a

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.730
versus just, like, a wrist flick.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.110
It's just personal preference.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.250
He actually has his holster
a little bit far back,

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:14.880
and he's pulling it out so far back

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.540
that he sort of stumbles this little bit

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:21.240
in order to get his gun
to go forward and shoot.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.260
If he had his gun where
I have it, more forward,

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:25.880
it would come out a lot easier.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:27.470
Jamie Foxx was thumbing.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:30.930
You can't just pull the trigger and fire.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:34.320
So, you're thumbing the gun
by pulling the hammer back.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.308
Then you pull the trigger,
and that fires it.

00:03:37.308 --> 00:03:38.683
[gunshot]

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:40.932
[gunshot]

00:03:40.932 --> 00:03:43.460
He was bringing the gun very up and down

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:45.593
and then really far out,

00:03:45.593 --> 00:03:47.320
which would be good for accuracy,

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:48.820
and he is doing point shooting,

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:50.866
so it works for this scene.

00:03:50.866 --> 00:03:52.382
[gunshots]

00:03:53.645 --> 00:03:54.970
In the second clip,

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:57.210
Jamie Foxx is actually fanning.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.660
So he is grabbing the gun

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:01.630
and he's using his opposite hand

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.170
and he's fanning the gun,

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:04.611
which actually is much quicker

00:04:04.611 --> 00:04:06.927
because you can hold down the trigger

00:04:06.927 --> 00:04:09.980
and use your opposing hand to fan the gun.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.662
And that would make it quicker.

00:04:11.662 --> 00:04:14.153
Versus thumbing each
one back individually,

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:16.480
one, two, one, two, one, two.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.613
He is doing it right,

00:04:17.613 --> 00:04:20.029
but the gun is obviously
quite heavy for him

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:22.235
because he's aiming downwards.

00:04:22.235 --> 00:04:25.610
See that little bit of weak wrist snap?

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:29.223
He's not shooting level.
He's shooting down.

00:04:29.223 --> 00:04:30.763
That might be because

00:04:30.763 --> 00:04:33.030
he can get down on the hammer easier

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.170
versus going straight across.

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:38.010
So, the gun that Jamie Foxx
is using is a thumbing gun.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:41.320
When it's a thumbing gun,
the hammer is back further.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:43.390
So you actually have to
come down on the gun.

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:46.290
If he had a fanning gun,
with the hammer forward,

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:47.800
it would be a lot easier to fan.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.850
You could actually go straight across.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:52.650
So, I typically use
this part of my finger,

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:55.247
which is my middle finger, and come down,

00:04:55.247 --> 00:04:57.718
instead of Jamie Foxx
is using his whole hand.

00:04:57.718 --> 00:05:00.242
You can get your soft tissues get caught,

00:05:00.242 --> 00:05:01.425
pinched in the gun,

00:05:01.425 --> 00:05:03.880
versus if you just have one clean sweep

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:05.680
with my one finger here,

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.800
it can go back a lot easier.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.300
I mean, the last shot was bomb.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.310
He did it in the Old Western style,

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.223
but the way he was doing it was pretty ...

00:05:14.223 --> 00:05:16.000
pretty decent. I would
definitely give him, like,

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.193
at least a seven out of 10.

00:05:18.193 --> 00:05:20.068
What else you got for me? Huh?

00:05:20.904 --> 00:05:23.750
[gunshots]

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:26.580
So, Tom Cruise is obviously
not using a revolver,

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.462
but his stance is actually very similar

00:05:28.462 --> 00:05:30.848
to what I would be doing in fast draw.

00:05:30.848 --> 00:05:33.620
His legs are over shoulder width apart.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:36.860
He has his right foot forward and bent.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:37.827
And when he shoots,

00:05:37.827 --> 00:05:42.202
he's actually bringing his
arm back and pinched in,

00:05:42.202 --> 00:05:43.940
which is very similar to me.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:45.573
He had his arm out, he grabs,

00:05:45.573 --> 00:05:47.339
he comes in, and he shoots.

00:05:47.339 --> 00:05:51.150
And then he does the typical
9-mil, I guess, style

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:54.330
of shooting out forward
for his second opponent.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.660
He does have his hips thrusted forward.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.243
He's actually bringing it into his side

00:05:59.243 --> 00:06:00.809
and locking the gun.

00:06:00.809 --> 00:06:02.560
So it's actually a very smooth draw.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.630
It's a little bit even
more similar to my style

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:08.090
than even those Old Western clips.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:09.273
Like, when I'm shooting,

00:06:09.273 --> 00:06:11.570
I have my one foot more forward.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:14.423
And then he goes, you know, like this.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.970
Where the old school, they're,
like, straight up and down,

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.650
and they're like --

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:21.950
You can tell that Tom
Cruise actually shoots.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:24.020
I personally think it was a great shot.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:25.295
I would actually probably give him

00:06:25.295 --> 00:06:26.628
a perfect 10 out of 10.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.152
[gunshots]

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:37.040
It looks as if he may already
have the gun cocked back,

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:38.830
and then when he goes to shoot,

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:42.670
he fires the gun and then pretends to fan.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.370
So it was actually quite bad.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:47.360
It's so quick that most
people would never notice.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.150
He also looks a little bit
sloppy when he's doing it.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:53.870
He's sort of, like,
hunched forward, like this,

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:56.793
and the gun is, like,
tipped up in the air.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:02.510
So it wasn't the smoothest
draw, by any means.

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:05.220
So, there's a huge
night-and-day difference

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.140
between the first clip of "Westworld"

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:11.403
and then James Marsden
actually practicing.

00:07:11.403 --> 00:07:12.426
[gunshot]

00:07:14.026 --> 00:07:15.382
[gunshots]

00:07:17.007 --> 00:07:18.220
[gunshots]

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.660
He is very straight up and down still,

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:25.138
but the third shot in
that practice sequence

00:07:25.138 --> 00:07:26.780
was really impressive,

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.930
because he thumbs the first one,

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:30.730
and then he fans the second one.

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:34.570
He went from being
awkward to more confident,

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:37.890
with a very clean stance and clean draw.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.610
The season one "Westworld,"

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:41.260
I probably would give that

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.640
more like a four out of 10.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:45.190
It was just sloppy.

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:47.065
There wasn't a lot of form.

00:07:47.065 --> 00:07:49.023
On the plus side, his training video

00:07:49.023 --> 00:07:52.340
I would almost give a 9.5 out of 10.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:55.283
It was crisp, clean, and
a lot of improvement.

00:08:00.574 --> 00:08:02.247
[blaster fire]

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:05.870
So, in this scene, as you can see,

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:08.003
the Mandalorian is really ready to shoot.

00:08:08.003 --> 00:08:09.838
He's sort of hovering over his gun,

00:08:09.838 --> 00:08:12.260
and he's just waiting
for the guy to get up.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:15.460
And as soon as the guy
moves, he grabs his gun,

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:18.660
and he just has a forward
motion for his shot.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:19.760
It's quite high.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:22.020
The draw itself was actually quite smooth.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:24.960
I just really liked that the
scene showed that he was,

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.660
like, waiting in anticipation
for his opponent.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:30.523
For him shooting with such a long gun,

00:08:30.523 --> 00:08:32.622
he does have a pretty good action.

00:08:32.622 --> 00:08:34.720
It's swift. It's going out forward.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:36.512
He locks it in.

00:08:36.512 --> 00:08:38.128
It's not easy to shoot a long gun,

00:08:38.128 --> 00:08:40.215
'cause it's a lot more pull

00:08:40.215 --> 00:08:41.581
to bring it out.

00:08:41.581 --> 00:08:42.790
The draw was actually pretty good

00:08:42.790 --> 00:08:43.940
for that style of gun

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:45.706
and this style of shooting.

00:08:46.914 --> 00:08:47.747
Nine.

00:08:52.068 --> 00:08:54.245
[gunshots]

00:08:54.245 --> 00:08:57.543
If you compare this to "Collateral,"

00:08:57.543 --> 00:09:00.295
Keanu Reeves is actually
grabbing with two hands

00:09:00.295 --> 00:09:02.170
almost pretty much right off the bat,

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:03.637
where ...

00:09:03.637 --> 00:09:06.637
so, that's the typical
shooting of a semiautomatic.

00:09:06.637 --> 00:09:09.514
So, the way that Keanu Reeves does it

00:09:09.514 --> 00:09:11.830
is he's shooting off his main foot.

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:15.300
The reason why is you can
typically get more movement

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.000
than when you're straight,

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:18.616
you can't really move as much.

00:09:18.616 --> 00:09:20.416
But there is different styles of shooting.

00:09:20.416 --> 00:09:22.610
It's lead foot, opposing foot,

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:25.605
or width is typically common.

00:09:25.605 --> 00:09:28.388
I just like that he's
going with his lead foot.

00:09:28.388 --> 00:09:30.732
I just think that it
makes it easier for him

00:09:30.732 --> 00:09:32.073
to pivot and turn.

00:09:32.073 --> 00:09:34.289
And it just, it looks really clean

00:09:34.289 --> 00:09:35.800
the way he's doing it.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:37.430
His front forward is a pivot.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:39.603
So he's shooting and pivoting around,

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.050
versus having to turn,
like, his whole body

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.540
and moving both feet left
and right, left and right.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.140
He can just use it as a pivot point.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.000
It looks really good.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.468
I could give him an 8.

00:09:54.286 --> 00:09:56.536
[gunshots]

00:09:58.349 --> 00:10:00.570
Clint Eastwood is doing a fanning style.

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:02.260
He's grabbing the gun,
and he's locking it,

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.990
and all he has to do is turn his body.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:06.475
It's always going to be level,

00:10:06.475 --> 00:10:09.115
because he has it locked into his side.

00:10:09.115 --> 00:10:12.348
So he goes bang, bang, bang.

00:10:12.348 --> 00:10:14.315
Tilts his body up. Bang.

00:10:14.315 --> 00:10:16.360
Tilts his body down. Bang.

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:19.550
The reason why you do this
is just more accuracy.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:23.370
Less flimsy wrist, less
poking it all around.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:24.350
When I'm fanning,

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:27.043
I actually thrust my hips forward,

00:10:27.043 --> 00:10:30.400
which gives me more of
an angle of my shooting.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:33.190
I have my shoulder out,
and it's more just a,

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:35.703
a pop and lock, pop and lock.

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:40.518
I do like that he locks it in

00:10:40.518 --> 00:10:42.850
to get his opposing targets,

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:44.666
versus using a sloppy wrist

00:10:44.666 --> 00:10:46.800
or moving his whole entire arm.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.620
So I do like that form.

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:50.410
That is this form that I use.

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:51.910
So a good nine out of 10.

00:10:53.969 --> 00:10:55.156
[Chewbacca roars]

00:10:55.156 --> 00:10:57.739
[blaster fire]

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.267
This is, like, the definition
of point and shoot.

00:11:02.267 --> 00:11:04.043
"There's my target! There I go!"

00:11:04.043 --> 00:11:05.650
People first start shooting,

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:06.960
they actually do this.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:08.299
And I'm like, "Oh, boy."

00:11:08.299 --> 00:11:11.690
Like, he's punching at his target,

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:13.200
he's going as far as he can,

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.470
and he's like, "Bang, I got you!"

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:18.350
So it's basically just
a beginner thing to do.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:19.830
When you jump forward and shoot,

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:22.900
you have a tendency to go low,

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:24.517
because you're, you know,

00:11:24.517 --> 00:11:26.700
you're almost going forward.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.500
It's just a natural reaction.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:31.380
It's not something that I
ever encourage people to do,

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.077
because it creates bad habits.

00:11:34.077 --> 00:11:35.640
The more you push forward,

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:37.310
the harder it will be to draw back.

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:38.680
Because once you get into a habit

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:40.660
of doing something, it's
super hard to break.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:44.030
He might be sort of turning to the side

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:45.470
to get away from shots.

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:47.612
Sometimes people will shoot to the side

00:11:47.612 --> 00:11:50.560
to make themselves slimmer.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:52.070
'Cause the more width you are,

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:54.828
the more target for somebody
shooting back at you.

00:11:54.828 --> 00:11:55.872
And if you turn to the side,

00:11:55.872 --> 00:11:57.860
you're a little bit harder to shoot.

00:11:57.860 --> 00:11:59.202
But he's not shooting back,

00:11:59.202 --> 00:12:01.960
so I don't really know why
he's shooting to the side.

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:03.210
As a quick-draw artist,

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:05.116
I would give this a tacky two.

00:12:05.116 --> 00:12:05.991
[laughs]

00:12:09.603 --> 00:12:11.463
[gunshot]

00:12:11.463 --> 00:12:12.990
It was all ugly.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:15.715
That was the pin that we're talking about,

00:12:15.715 --> 00:12:17.331
the firing pin.

00:12:17.331 --> 00:12:19.520
See how his finger's almost touching it?

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:22.150
A lot of people don't like those guns

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:24.250
for the fact that you could catch it.

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:26.220
But for fanning, you
wouldn't want to use one

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:28.380
with a firing pin exposed,

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.880
because you could actually
poke your finger with it.

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:32.710
The actual thumbing itself,

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:34.820
the way he grabbed the hammer back

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:36.470
and thumbed the gun,

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:38.270
looked really, really good.

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:40.900
But then, all of a sudden,
it's like a split clip,

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:44.150
and then you see him sort of jolt up

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:46.750
and messily trying to shoot under his arm.

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:48.190
It's just unrealistic.

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:49.677
Especially if you're thumbing the gun,

00:12:49.677 --> 00:12:52.200
to have your hand in front
and try to shoot under it,

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:53.983
you might shoot your arm off.

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:56.523
I didn't like it.

00:12:56.523 --> 00:13:00.834
I thought it was, like,
really messy, unsafe.

00:13:00.834 --> 00:13:02.939
I feel like he could have
done it a lot better.

00:13:02.939 --> 00:13:05.089
I would say he's probably gonna get, like,

00:13:05.089 --> 00:13:06.005
a three.

00:13:07.709 --> 00:13:10.376
[hammer clicking]
[slapping face]

00:13:12.649 --> 00:13:15.240
He's definitely throwing
him off with his left hand.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:16.788
You watch the hand that moves first,

00:13:16.788 --> 00:13:19.150
so he has a good tactic on that one.

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:22.320
It comes out of the
holster fairly smoothly,

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:24.220
but that's the reason why
it's coming out of the holster

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:27.450
really smoothly, is because
it's a lowered holster.

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:29.360
One of the reasons why
his holster might be lower

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:33.120
is it gives some more
options to move around.

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:35.160
But his draw has no, like, accuracy form.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:38.190
So good thing the guy's only,
like, a foot away from him.

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:40.820
He is doing an all-arm motion.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:42.829
There's no lock at all to it.

00:13:42.829 --> 00:13:44.753
And when he does that, if you notice,

00:13:44.753 --> 00:13:47.303
he butts down his wrist,

00:13:47.303 --> 00:13:50.280
and he actually has his
gun sort of tilted upwards.

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:52.973
So when he goes to draw,

00:13:52.973 --> 00:13:56.180
he goes all arm and he butts it down.

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:57.910
So, with all shooting sports,

00:13:57.910 --> 00:14:00.330
it's actually very important
to have strong wrists.

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.420
I have incredibly strong wrists.

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:05.270
So no matter how I fan,

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:08.653
my wrist is always locked and firm.

00:14:08.653 --> 00:14:10.430
I'd probably rate it a five out of 10.

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:12.138
It doesn't come out badly,

00:14:12.138 --> 00:14:14.638
he's not doing a lot badly,

00:14:14.638 --> 00:14:17.963
it's just that he doesn't
have a lot of accuracy.

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:20.770
It isn't the worst.

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:21.880
It isn't the best.

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:24.300
It's just mediocre.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:26.970
It is the safest gun sport in the world,

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:29.600
'cause we're shooting wax
or black powder blanks.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:31.700
That's why we can shoot as fast as we can.

